 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "Why does your shower get weak at seven in the morning?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "It's not your shower. It's the pipe under your street.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Water reaches your home through a big pipe called a water main.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A pump far away pushes it, and that push is the pressure you feel.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "At three in the morning, almost nobody uses water, so the push is strong.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But at seven, thousands of people shower, flush and make coffee at the same time.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The faster water moves, the more it rubs against the pipe walls.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That friction eats the pressure, a little more every block.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So the houses at the far end of the line feel it first.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "That's why cities build water towers and hilltop tanks.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "At night, pumps fill them up while water is cheap and nobody needs it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In the morning rush, gravity pushes that stored water back into the pipes.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "The tank covers the peak, so the pumps don't have to.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And if your shower is still weak, your house might simply be too far, or too high.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Now you know.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
